  "nl_text": "The database schema named \"schemabench_0002\" consists of 11 tables.\nThe \"users\" table represents a collection of users, with each user identified by a unique id. The avatar, role_id, and settings fields are optional and can contain various data types. The role_id field references the id in the \"roles\" table.\nThe \"data_types\" table is used to define various data types. Each data type is identified by a unique id, and has various attributes such as name, slug, display names, icon, model name, policy name, controller, description, generate permissions, server-side status, details, and timestamps for creation and update.\nThe \"menus\" and \"menu_items\" tables are used to manage navigation menus. Each menu is identified by a unique id and has timestamps for creation and update. The \"menu_items\" table references the \"menus\" table through the menu_id field.\nThe \"roles\" table represents user roles. Each role is identified by a unique id and has a name and display name.\nThe \"settings\" table is used to manage various settings. Each setting is identified by a unique id, has a key, display name, value, details, type, order, and group.\nThe \"permissions\" table is used to manage permissions. Each permission is identified by a unique id and has a key, table name, and timestamps for creation and update.\nThe \"permission_role\" table is a junction table that links permissions and roles. Each record in this table has a permission_id and a role_id, which reference the ids in the \"permissions\" and \"roles\" tables respectively.\nThe \"translations\" table is used to manage translations. Each translation is identified by a unique id, has a table name, column name, foreign key, locale, and value.\nThe \"data_rows\" table is used to manage data rows. Each data row is identified by a unique id, has a data type id, field, type, display name, required, browse, read, edit, add, delete, details, and order. The data type id references the id in the \"data_types\" table.\nFinally, the \"user_roles\" table is a junction table that links users and roles. Each record in this table has a user_id and a role_id, which reference the ids in the \"users\" and \"roles\" tables respectively. This schema appears to be a comprehensive solution for managing user roles, permissions, and data types, as well as translations and rows.",
  "gold_question": "Background / Context\n\nVoyager is an admin panel package for Laravel, developed by The Control Group. It provides a rapid scaffolding solution for backend management, simplifying common administrative tasks within Laravel applications. The package is designed to enhance usability for developers by offering pre-built functionalities for managing app data and user interfaces, thereby reducing development time and effort.\n\n Objectives\n\nThe primary objective of Voyager is to streamline the development of admin interfaces for Laravel applications. It aims to provide:\n- A cohesive admin interface for managing application data.\n- Easily configurable tools for creating and handling BREAD/CRUD operations.\n- Efficient media and menu management functionalities.\n\n Stakeholders & Users\n\n- Developers: Primary users who integrate Voyager into their Laravel applications to manage backend admin tasks.\n- Admin Users: Users who access the admin panel to perform data management, media handling, and menu building.\n- The Control Group: Developers and maintainers of the Voyager package.\n\n Functional Descriptions\n\n1. Admin Interface: Allows users to add, edit, and delete app data. \n2. Menu Builder: Facilitates creation and management of site menus, allowing for the addition, editing, and deletion of items across various menu locations such as header, sidebar, and footer.\n3. Media Manager: Provides capabilities for uploading, renaming, and deleting files. Users can also create and organize folders.\n4. CRUD/BREAD Builder: Enables the generation of CRUD operations and BREAD functionalities across application tables. \n5. User Roles and Permissions: Manages user roles and permissions to access different admin functionalities.\n6. Data Type Management: Supports defining and managing different data types and their attributes.\n\n Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Performance: The admin panel should handle operations efficiently to ensure a smooth user experience without unnecessary delays.\n- Scalability: The system should support scaling as the application data and user base grow.\n- Security: All data interactions and file management operations should be secured to prevent unauthorized access and data breaches.\n- Usability: The interface should be intuitive and easy for both developers and admin users to navigate and operate.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_deepseek/0002.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0002\nCREATE TABLE `users` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `avatar` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `role_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `settings` json DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `users_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`role_id`) REFERENCES `roles` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `data_types` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `slug` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `display_names` json DEFAULT NULL,\n  `icon` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `model_name` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `policy_name` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `controller` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `generate_permissions` json DEFAULT NULL,\n  `server_side_status` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `details` json DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `menus` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `menu_items` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `menu_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `menu_items_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`menu_id`) REFERENCES `menus` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `roles` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `display_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `settings` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `key` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `display_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `value` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `details` json DEFAULT NULL,\n  `type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `order` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `group` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `permissions` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `key` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `table_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `permission_role` (\n  `permission_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `role_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`permission_id`, `role_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `permission_role_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`permission_id`) REFERENCES `permissions` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `permission_role_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`role_id`) REFERENCES `roles` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `translations` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `table_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `column_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `foreign_key` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `locale` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `value` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `data_rows` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `data_type_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `field` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `type` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `display_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `required` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `browse` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `read` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `edit` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `add` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `delete` tinyint(1) NOT NULL,\n  `details` json DEFAULT NULL,\n  `order` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `data_rows_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`data_type_id`) REFERENCES `data_types` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `user_roles` (\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `role_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`user_id`, `role_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `user_roles_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `user_roles_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`role_id`) REFERENCES `roles` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
